<h1><a name="_Toc32491847"><span style='font-size:12.0pt'>18.17  GREEN BUILDING
PROGRAM</span></a></h1>

<p class=open style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=open style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>Sections:</span></b></p>

<p class=tx style='margin-left:1.5in;text-indent:-1.5in'><span
style='font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          18.17.010     Definitions.</span></p>

<p class=tx style='margin-left:1.5in;text-indent:-1.5in'><span
style='font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          18.17.025     Policy
intent - applicability.</span></p>

<p class=tx style='margin-left:1.5in;text-indent:-1.5in'><span
style='font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          18.17.030     Support
- management - grant requirement.</span></p>

<p class=tx style='margin-left:1.5in;text-indent:-1.5in'><span
style='font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          18.17.040     Lighting
purchases - energy-efficient-outdoor light pollution reduction.</span></p>

<p class=tx style='margin-left:1.5in;text-indent:-1.5in'><span
style='font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          18.17.050     Standards
and requirements for capital projects, leases - county-owned historic landmarks
and properties preservation and restoration.</span></p>

<p class=tx style='margin-left:1.5in;text-indent:-1.5in'><span
style='font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          18.17.060     Requirements
for implementing agencies for capital projects, capital projects involving
landmark-designation-eligible landmarks or properties.</span></p>

<p class=tx style='margin-left:1.5in;text-indent:-1.5in'><span
style='font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          18.17.070     Biennial
report on progress of implementing chapter - reports by capital project
managers - annual provision of Evergreen Sustainable Development Standards by
department of community and human services.</span></p>

<p class=tx style='margin-left:1.5in;text-indent:-1.5in'><span
style='font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          18.17.080     Operational
guidelines - development by green building team.</span></p>

<p class=tx style='margin-left:1.5in;text-indent:-1.5in'><span
style='font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          18.17.090     Green
building team duties - designation of green building team members by divisions
- interagency review committee.</span></p>

<p class=tx style='margin-left:1.5in;text-indent:-1.5in'><span
style='font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          18.17.100     Technical
support by GreeenTools program.</span></p>

<p class=tx style='margin-left:1.5in;text-indent:-1.5in'><span
style='font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          18.17.110     Ordinances
and strategies to increase sustainable development, awareness, certification
and innovation in green building.</span></p>

<p class=tx><span style='font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>          <b>18.17.010  Definitions.</b>  The
definitions in this section apply throughout this chapter unless the context
clearly requires otherwise.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>          A.  &quot;Alternative green building
rating system&quot; means a third-party green building certification other than
LEED or the King County Sustainable Infrastructure Scorecard.  The following
are accepted alternative green building rating systems, but the executive may
also accept certification through other rating systems as appropriate:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            1.  Built Green Four-Star, Built
Green Five-Star, or Built Green Emerald Star, or any combination thereof;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            2.  Envision;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            3.  Evergreen Sustainable
Development Standard;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            4.  Fitwel;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            5.  Greenroads;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            6.  Living Building Challenge;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            7.  Passive House;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            8.  Salmon Safe;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            9.  SITES; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            10.  WELL.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>          B.  &quot;Built Green
Four-Star,&quot; &quot;Built Green Five-Star,&quot; and &quot;Built Green
Emerald Star&quot; mean a third-party residential green building certification
developed and administered by the Master Builders Association of King and
Snohomish Counties.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>          C.  &quot;Capital project&quot; means
capital project as defined in K.C.C. 4A.10.100.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>          D.  &quot;Energy Star&quot; means the
energy certification rating system developed by the United States Environmental
Protection Agency that focuses on energy efficiency.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>          E.  &quot;Envision&quot; means a
voluntary sustainable infrastructure rating system administered by the
Institute for Sustainable Infrastructure and developed by the Harvard
University Graduate School of Design, American Public Works Association,
American Society of Civil Engineers, and the American Council of Engineering
Companies for assessing sustainability and resilience in infrastructure.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>          F.  &quot;Equity&quot; means equity
as defined in K.C.C. 2.10.210.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>          G.  &quot;Equity and social justice
credits&quot; means credits awarded through the Sustainable Infrastructure
Scorecard for actions that identify and account for equity and social justice
practices and outcomes throughout the capital project development lifecycle. 
The credits recognize project team efforts to advance process, distributional
and cross-generational equity. </span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>          H.  &quot;Evergreen Sustainable
Development Standard&quot; means a sustainable building program for affordable
housing projects that receive housing trust funds, administered by the
Washington state Department of Commerce according to RCW 39.35D.080.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>          I.  &quot;Facility&quot; means all or
any portion of buildings, structures, infrastructure, sites, complexes,
equipment, utilities and conveyance lines.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>          J.  &quot;Fitwel&quot; means a
third-party green building rating system administered by the Center for Active
Design that provides a standard that supports health-promoting strategies in
the built environment.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>          K. &quot;Green building team&quot;
means a group that includes representatives from county agencies with capital
project or building management staff including, but not limited to, the Metro
transit department, the department of natural resources and parks, the
department of executive services, the department of local services, permitting
and road services divisions, the public health - Seattle &amp; King County, the
historic preservation program, and the department of community and human
services.  The members represent staff with expertise in project management,
construction management, architecture, landscape architecture, environmental
planning, design, engineering, historic preservation and resource conservation,
public health, building energy systems, building management, budget analysis,
equity and racial and social justice, procurement, and other skills as needed. 
The green building team provides assistance and helps to disseminate
information to project managers in all county agencies.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>          L.  &quot;Greenroads&quot; means the
third-party green building rating system administered by the Greenroads
International nonprofit organization to measure and manage sustainability on
transportation projects.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>          M.  &quot;GreenTools program&quot;
means the support team located within the solid waste division of the
department of natural resources and parks that provides green building
technical assistance to county divisions, cities, and the general public within
the county.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>          N.  &quot;Integrative process&quot;
means an approach to project design that seeks to achieve high performance on a
wide variety of well-defined environmental and social goals while staying
within budgetary and scheduling constraints.  It relies on a multidisciplinary
and collaborative team whose members make decisions together based on a shared
vision and a holistic understanding of the project.  It is an iterative process
that follows the design through the entire project life, from predesign through
operation.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>          O.  &quot;Leadership in Energy and
Environmental Design&quot; or &quot;LEED&quot; means a voluntary,
consensus-based national standard for developing high-performance, sustainable
buildings, created by the United States Green Building Council.  The
certification options are LEED Gold, LEED Platinum, Zero Energy, and Zero
Carbon.</span></p>

<p class=level2 style='margin:0in;text-align:justify'><span style='font-family:
"Arial",sans-serif'>          P.  &quot;LEED-eligible building&quot; means any
new construction or major remodel or renovation capital project with one
thousand gross square feet or more of new, remodeled or renovated floor area
that is occupied or conditioned and that meets the minimum program requirements
for LEED certifications.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify;text-autospace:none'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>          Q. 
&quot;Living Building Challenge&quot; means a voluntary green building rating
system administered by the International Living Future Institute.  The
certification options are Full Living, Petal, CORE, Zero Energy, and Zero
Carbon.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify;text-autospace:none'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>          R. 
&quot;Major remodel or renovation&quot; means work that demolishes space down
to the shell structure and rebuilds it with new interior walls, ceilings, floor
coverings, and systems, when the work affects more than twenty-five percent of
a building's square footage and the affected space is one thousand square feet
or larger.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>          S.  &quot;Minor remodel or
renovation&quot; means any type of remodel or renovation that does not qualify
as a major remodel or renovation.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>          T.  &quot;New construction&quot;
means a new building or structure.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>          U.  &quot;Passive House&quot; means a
voluntary passive building energy standard certification program through either
the PHIUS+ certification administered by Passive House Institute United States
or the Passive House certification administered by Passive House Institute.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>          V.  &quot;Regional code
collaboration&quot; means interested jurisdictions across the Puget Sound
region working together to develop building, energy, fire, residential,
plumbing, mechanical, and zoning codes supporting the advancement of green
building practices.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>          W.  &quot;Retrocommissioning&quot; means
a detailed, systematic process for investigating an existing building's
operations and identifying ways to improve performance.  The primary focus is
to identify operational improvements to obtain comfort and energy savings.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>          X.  &quot;Salmon Safe&quot; means a
voluntary peer-reviewed certification program, linking site development land
management practices with the protection of agricultural and urban watersheds,
founded by the Stewardship Partners.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>          Y.  &quot;SITES&quot; means a
voluntary sustainability-focused framework program administered by the
Sustainable SITES Initiative and developed by the American Society of Landscape
Architects, the Lady Bird Johnson Wildflower Center, and the United States
Botanical Garden.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>          Z.  &quot;Social cost of carbon&quot;
means social cost of carbon as defined in K.C.C. 18.20.015.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>          AA.  &quot;Social justice&quot; means
social justice as defined in K.C.C. 2.10.210.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>          BB.  &quot;Strategic Climate Action
Plan&quot; means the King County Strategic Climate Action Plan adopted by
Motion 15866, or any subsequent Strategic Climate Action Plan developed under
K.C.C. chapter 18.25 and adopted by the council.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>          CC.  &quot;Sustainable development
practices&quot; are also known as green building and means whole system
approaches to the design, construction, and operation of buildings and
infrastructure that help to mitigate the negative environmental, economic,
health, and social impacts of construction, demolition, operation, and
renovation while maximizing the facilities' positive fiscal, environmental,
health, and functional contribution.  Sustainable development practices
recognize the relationship between natural and built environments and seek to
minimize the use of energy, water, and other natural resources while providing
maximum benefits and contribution to service levels to the system and the
connecting infrastructures.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>          DD.  &quot;Sustainable Infrastructure
Scorecard&quot; means a green building and sustainable development rating
system developed by the green building team for capital projects that are not
eligible for the LEED rating system.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>          EE.  &quot;Transit-oriented
development&quot; means a capital project on King County-owned property that
includes the development of housing, commercial space, services, or job
opportunities in direct proximity to frequent public transportation and that is
wholly or partially planned or wholly or partially financed by the Metro
transit department.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>          FF.  &quot;WELL&quot; means a
third-party green building rating system administered by the International WELL
Building Institute's collaboration with Green Business Certification, Inc.  (Ord.
19986 § 3, 2025:  Ord. 19881 § 27, 2024:  Ord. 19402 § 5, 2022:  Ord. 18791 § 137,
2018:  Ord. 18777 § 32, 2018:  Ord. 17709 § 2, 2013:  Ord. 17420 § 75, 2012: 
Ord. 16147 <span style='letter-spacing:-.1pt'>§</span> 2, 2008.  Formerly
K.C.C. 2.95.005).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>'</span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>''''''''<strong><span style='font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
font-weight:normal'>    </span></strong><strong><span style='font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>18.17.025 
Policy intent - applicability.</span></strong></span></p>

<p class=open style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>          A. 
The policy intent of this chapter is to ensure that the planning, design,
construction, remodeling, renovation, historic preservation, maintenance,
operation and decommissioning of any King County-owned, lease-to-own or
financed capital project is consistent with the latest green building and
sustainable development practices.</span></p>

<p class=open style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>          B. 
The policy intent is to improve energy efficiency, reduce greenhouse gas
emissions, advance equity and social justice, reduce waste, reduce water use,
increase sustainable materials use, improve sites and improve stormwater
management.</span></p>

<p class=open style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>          C. 
This chapter applies to all King County-owned or lease-to-own capital projects,
excluding projects that have already completed thirty percent of the design
phase by March 21, 2022.</span></p>

<p class=open style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>             D. 
This chapter also applies to certain housing projects partly or totally
financed by King County that are required by law to follow statewide green
building standards.  (Ord. 19402 § 7, 2022).</span></p>

<p class=open style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><strong><span style='font-size:
12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;font-weight:normal'>          </span></strong><strong><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>18.17.030  Support -
management - grant requirements.</span></strong></p>

<p style='margin:0in;text-align:justify'><strong><span style='font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
font-weight:normal'>          </span></strong><span style='font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>A. 
The department of natural resources and parks shall continue the green building
grant program established to provide incentives to the private sector,
nonprofit organizations and suburban cities to adopt green building and
sustainable development practices.</span></p>

<p style='margin:0in;text-align:justify'><span style='font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>          B. 
Grant funding shall be supported by the solid waste division, the water and
land resources division and the wastewater treatment division.  Other county
department and divisions may also participate in the grant program.  Grant
funding shall be identified annually, consistent with approved funding of each
division's annual budget.</span></p>

<p style='margin:0in;text-align:justify'><span style='font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>          C. 
Grant funds shall be managed by the GreenTools program in cooperation with the
wastewater treatment and water and land resources divisions.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>          D.  Green building grant funding may
go to residential or commercial projects that meet a discrete set of
eligibility requirements, are in the service area of the division providing the
grant funding and are selected in a competitive award process.  Grant projects
must provide educational opportunities to the public to increase the awareness
and benefits of green building and sustainable development in King County. 
(Ord. 16147 <span style='letter-spacing:-.1pt'>§</span> 4, 2008.  Formerly
K.C.C. 2.95.025).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>          <b>18.17.040  Lighting purchases -
energy-efficient-outdoor light pollution reduction.</b></span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>          A.  King County shall purchase the
most cost-effective and energy-efficient lighting feasible and seek available
financial incentives from appropriate entities, including but not limited to
utilities.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>          B.  All lighting purchases for the
county for which there is a federal efficiency standard established in 10 CFR
Sec. 430.32 (2009) shall meet or exceed those energy efficiency standards to
the maximum extent feasible. The department director or designee shall make the
determination as to whether it is feasible to meet these standards.  In making
a determination concerning the feasibility of installing lighting that complies
with the federal efficiency standards, the department director or designee shall
consider:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            1.  The life-cycle cost
effectiveness of the lighting</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            2.  The compatibility of the
lighting with existing equipment;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            3.  Whether use of the lighting
could result in interference with productivity or safety, and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            4.  The aesthetics relating to the
use of the lighting.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>          C.  Any new outdoor lighting
installed on a county facility, that has output greater than one thousand eight
hundred lumens must be fully shielded, except as exempted in subsection E. of
this section.  For the purposes of this section, &quot;fully shielded&quot;
means the outdoor lighting fixture is one in which all light rays are projected
below a horizontal plane running through the lowest point on the fixture where
light is emitted.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>          D.  Beginning July 1, 2015, all
replacement lighting with output greater than one thousand eight hundred lumens
on county facilities must be fully shielded, except as exempted in subsection
E. of this section.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>          E.  An action for reducing light
pollution shall not be implemented if it is determined by the department
director or designee that the action will reduce lighting deemed necessary for
public safety.  Further, the following lighting categories are exempted from
this section:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            1.  Temporary lighting for
construction projects;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            2.  Temporary emergency lighting
used by police, fire departments or other emergency services;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            3.  Hazard warning lights required
by federal law and regulatory agencies;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            4.  Seasonal and decorative
lighting displays that use multiple low wattage bulbs</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            5.  Navigation lights used for
marine and aviation safety;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            6.  Historical buildings that meet
the requirements of RCW 19.27.120</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            7.  Road lighting if shielded
lighting is in conflict with state or federal requirements;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            8.  Ball field lights, but only if
the department of natural resources and parks has taken steps to minimize glare
and light trespass; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            9.  Exit signs, lighting for stairs
and ramps.  (Ord. 16769 <span style='letter-spacing:-.1pt'>1, </span>2010. 
Formerly K.C.C. 2.95.035).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>          <b>18.17.050  Standards and
requirements for capital projects, leases - county-owned historic landmarks and
properties preservation and restoration.</b></span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>          A.  Capital projects shall be subject
to the following applicable green building standards and corresponding
requirements; capital projects shall register with the applicable third-party
rating system and achieve the appropriate certification:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            1.  Small, related capital projects
that are part of a program may be certified as a program rather than at the
individual-project level;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            2.a.  Affordable housing capital
projects subject to RCW 39.35D.080 that receive moneys from the King County department
of community and human services or that are part of transit-oriented
development shall achieve either Evergreen Sustainable Development Standard
requirements or the highest rating in an applicable alternative green building
rating system certification, or both; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              b.  Market-rate transit-oriented
development on county-owned property shall achieve either LEED platinum
certification or Living Building Challenge CORE certification;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            3.  Buildings owned or lease-to-own
by King County, excluding those to which subsection A.2. of this section
applies, shall achieve certification levels as follows:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              a.  New construction of a
LEED-eligible building shall achieve:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>                (1) either LEED platinum
certification or the Living Building Challenge certification, or both, if the
project is initiated before January 1, 2026; or</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>                (2)  either LEED Platinum plus
Zero Energy or Zero Carbon certification, or Living Building Challenge CORE
plus Zero Energy, Energy Petal, or Zero Carbon Certification, if the project is
initiated on or after January 1, 2026;; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              b.  A major remodel or renovation
of a LEED-eligible building shall achieve either LEED gold certification or the
Living Building Challenge certification, or both;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            4.  Capital projects owned or
lease-to-own by King County that are large-scale infrastructure or site-related
projects shall achieve Envision Platinum certification.  This requirement is
limited to: new construction of conveyance, combined sewer overflow, and pump station
projects from the wastewater treatment division that are over a cost threshold
established by executive policy; new RapidRide and bus base electrification
charging infrastructure projects by Metro transit department; and heavy-duty
electrical infrastructure projects with ten or more direct-current fast
chargers by the solid waste division;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            5.  Capital projects owned or
lease-to-own by King County that are not subject to subsection A. 2., 3., or 4.
of this section shall either achieve a platinum rating according to a King
County or division-specific Sustainable Infrastructure Scorecard or achieve the
highest certification through an applicable alternative green building rating
system, or both; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            6.  The parks and recreation division
shall maintain its overall, programmatic Salmon Safe certification.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>          B.  All capital projects to which
subsection A. of this section applies:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            1.  Shall meet King County Surface
Water Design Manual requirements, regardless of jurisdiction location.  If a
project is located in a jurisdiction where the surface water design manual
standards and requirements are different than King County's, the project shall
implement the more stringent requirement;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            2.  Shall achieve a minimum
diversion rate for construction and demolition materials of eighty-five percent
and shall achieve zero waste of resources with economic value beginning in
2030;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            3.  Shall achieve applicable King
County equity and social justice credits for capital projects regardless of the
rating system used;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            4.  Should use the practice of
integrative process to maximize green building, sustainable development,
community benefit, and financial investment opportunities over the life of the
asset; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            5.  Should use the social cost of
carbon in life-cycle assessments and decision making related to facility
construction and resource efficiency projects.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>          C.1.  For leases by a King County
agency for King County operations at non-King-County-owned facilities, the
agency shall seek to incorporate the latest green building and sustainable
development practices in the county-occupied space.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            2.  For new leases of King
County-employee-occupied space of longer than five years, including
lease-to-own projects, King County shall lease buildings that are certified
through the LEED rating system at silver level or higher, are Energy Star
Certified or are certified through an alternative green building rating system,
but only when those ratings are consistent with the operational needs of the
function.  Buildings that do not meet these standards can be leased by the
county if plans and financing are in place at the time of signing that will
enable the building to meet this standard within twenty-four months of lease
signing.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>          D.  As part of the county's green
building program, the county shall preserve and restore the historic landmarks
and properties eligible for landmark designation that are owned by the county,
except in cases where a certificate of appropriateness is granted by the King
County landmarks commission.  (Ord. 19986 § 4, 2025:  Ord. 19881 § 28, 2024:  Ord.
19402 § 8, 2022).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>          <b>18.17.060  Requirements for
implementing agencies for capital projects, capital projects involving
landmark-designation-eligible landmarks or properties.</b></span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>          A.  Implementing agencies for capital
projects subject to K.C.C. 18.17.050.A. shall comply with the requirements in
this chapter while balancing fiscal stewardship, community investment and
environmental excellence over the life of the asset.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>          B.1.  The implementing agency for a
capital project may apply to the executive or designee for a waiver to some or
all of this chapter if, in the agency's judgement, compliance is not possible,
the costs of compliance are unreasonable or the costs of compliance do not
generate sufficient fiscal, operational, environmental and social benefits to
justify the costs.  The implementing agency should apply for a waiver at or
before thirty-percent design.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            2.  When reviewing applications for
waivers, the executive or designee may consider:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              a.  the costs and benefits of
full compliance over the life of the asset, including environmental and equity
benefits;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              b.  whether it is possible for
the project to comply; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              c.  the degree to which the
project will take steps in lieu of full compliance to:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>                (1)  mitigate and avoid
negative environmental impacts;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>                (2)  reduce greenhouse gas
emissions; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>                (3)  address inequities in the
distribution of resources and access to a healthy build and natural
environment.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            3.  If the executive or designee
grants a waiver to a requirement in this chapter, the executive shall transmit
in the quarterly budget management report required by K.C.C. 4A.100.100 a list
of projects receiving the waiver, the reasons for the waiver and any actions
the implementing agency will take to meet the policy intent of the chapter
within reasonable costs.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>          C.   Capital projects involving
designated landmarks or properties that are eligible for landmark designation
shall seek to maximize green building strategies appropriate to the project. 
However, in addition to the process described in subsection B. of this section,
the King County landmarks commission or King County historic preservation
officer may also waive some or all of the requirements of this chapter for
these capital projects upon issuing findings that strict compliance would
adversely affect the historic character of the resource in question or there
are no feasible alternatives for preservation.  (Ord. 19402 § 9, 2022).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>          <b>18.17.070  Biennial report on
progress of implementing chapter - reports by capital project managers - annual
provision of Evergreen Sustainable Development Standards by department of
community and human services.</b></span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>          A.  Except for capital projects
subject to subsection B. of this section, the capital project manager for each
project to which this chapter applies shall report the following information to
the green building team; small, related projects that are certified as a
program may report as a program:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            1.  By January 31 following each
year a project is active, an annual green building report capturing
sustainability and green building efforts;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            2.  At thirty-percent design phase
completion:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              a.  a Sustainable Infrastructure
Scorecard, LEED or alternative green building rating system checklist.  The
checklist shall document which rating system and certification level the
project is planned to achieve;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              b.  a construction and demolition
material diversion plan; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              c.  a list of the equity and
social justice credits the project will pursue; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            3.  At project completion:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              a.  an updated LEED, Sustainable
Infrastructure Scorecard or alternative green building rating system
checklist.  The checklist shall document which rating system points and
certification level the project actually achieved;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              b.  a construction and demolition
material diversion report; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              c.  a list of the equity and
social justice credits the project implemented.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>          B.  By January 31 of each year,
designees from the department of community and human services shall provide the
green building team with the Evergreen Sustainable Development Standard
checklist for each housing project subject to K.C.C. 18.17.050.A.2.a. that was
awarded funding in the previous year.  (Ord. 19986 § 5, 2025:  Ord. 19402 § 10,
2022).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>          <b>18.17.080  Operational guidelines
- development by green building team.</b>  To help achieve a standard level of
green building operations in existing buildings, the green building team shall
develop a set of both mandatory and recommended green building operational
guidelines for divisions to incorporate into their facility operations
procedures.  The development shall be in coordination with divisions that have
capital project or building management staff and the GreenTools technical
support team.  Each division shall identify priorities for incorporating new
green operations and maintenance practices in its line of business.  The
guidelines shall provide direction on the use of green practices in minor
remodels and renovations, water and energy conservation, waste reduction and
recycling expectations, sustainable material procurement, green cleaning
standards and retrocommissioning to improve a facility's operating
performance.  County divisions shall have flexibility to select standards most
applicable to their line of business, either King County's green operations and
maintenance guidelines handbook or an existing third-party standard.  (Ord.
19402 § 11, 2022).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>          <b>18.17.090  Green building team
duties - designation of green building team members by divisions - interagency
review committee.</b></span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>          A.  The green building team shall
coordinate and share information about the use of sustainable development
practices countywide and, with assistance from the GreenTools program, develop
tools and training for project managers to implement this chapter.  The green
building team's duties include:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            1.  Helping to assess regionally
appropriate green building and sustainable development practices;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            2.  Developing regionally
appropriate building and facility design standards and guidelines;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            3.  Developing tools and procedures
for assessing life-cycle fiscal, environmental and functional costs and
benefits;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            4.  Convening and facilitating
sustainable development planning and charrette workshops;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            5.  Evaluating performance of
projects and facilities, including conducting post occupancy surveys, energy
and water use audits and evaluating benefits realized; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            6.  Tracking and reporting progress
on implementation of green building and sustainable development practices.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>          B.  Each division with capital
project, operations and maintenance, building management, permitting or housing
staff shall designate one or more green building team member.  The green
building team member shall regularly attend meetings and actively participate
in disseminating sustainable development practices information back to the
respective division.  Green building team members may also receive specialized
training in green building design and should be encouraged to achieve green
building professional credentials.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>          C.  The green building team shall
assist with formation of an interagency review committee with members from
permitting agencies, including the department of public health, water and land
resources division of the department of natural resources and parks, department
of local services and the green building team, to facilitate review of projects
that involve multiple green building systems and to facilitate approval of
buildings using high performance rating systems or features.  (Ord. 19402 § 12,
2022).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>          <b>18.17.100  Technical support by
GreeenTools program.</b>  The GreenTools program shall provide technical
support for the green building team and to cities and the general public as
appropriate.  Technical support should include, but not be limited to: </span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>          A.  Training on LEED and other green
building and sustainable development certifications and technologies;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>          B.  Research;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>          C.  Project review;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>          D.  Assisting with budget analysis;
and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>          E.  Convening groups to develop
strategies and policies relating to green buildings and sustainable development
practices.  (Ord. 19402 § 13, 2022).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>          <b>18.17.110  Ordinances and
strategies to increase sustainable development, awareness, certification and
innovation in green building.</b>  To increase green building practices on a
community-wide level, King County shall amend King County ordinances and
implement strategies that increase sustainable development, awareness,
certification and innovation in green building, informed by the priority
actions and targets of the King County Strategic Climate Action Plan.  Efforts
shall include, but not be limited to, the following:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>          A.  The department of local services,
permitting division, shall develop educational materials on sustainable
practices and techniques for green building and site development, such as the
green building handbook.  The educational materials shall apply to new
construction, additions, remodels and renovation projects in unincorporated
King County, and shall be developed and provided in such a way that all county
residents have access to the information.  The permitting division shall also
develop guidance materials and coordinate training that informs county staff on
how to review and inspect green building techniques throughout the development
review process;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>          B.  The department of public health,
the water and land resources division of the department of natural resources
and parks and the department of local services, permitting division, shall
ensure that all staff who review and inspect development permits receive
training in green building and high-performance rating systems, such as Built
Green Emerald Star, LEED Platinum and the International Living Future Institute's
Living Building Challenge certifications;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>          C.  The department of local services,
permitting division, shall participate in the regional code collaboration to
develop and unify building codes throughout jurisdictions in the Puget Sound
region that promote green building and supports shared economic growth and
sustainability.  The county shall promote amendments developed by the regional
code collaboration for inclusion in the Washington State Energy Code. 
Amendments developed by the regional code collaboration shall be adopted, when
appropriate, in the county's building and construction codes, with initial
emphasis on minimum recycling requirements for construction and demolition
projects;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>          D.  The county, in partnership with
other public and private entities, shall provide assistance to increase the
development of green affordable housing, green building certifications,
efficiency upgrades in existing buildings and stronger state and local
standards for new and existing buildings;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>          E.  The department of local services,
permitting division, shall research and, as viable, propose additional King
County zoning and building ordinances, policies, incentives and programs to
support the advancement of countywide green building efforts; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>          F.  The procurement services section
of the department of executive services shall, where possible and appropriate,
include green building requirements in capital design and construction
contracts, bid documents and technical specifications.  The project manager
responsible for the capital project shall collaborate with procurement services
section staff to determine where green building requirements are possible and
appropriate.  As applicable, requests for proposals and qualifications should
include a list or description of green building rating system certification
experience.  Procurement documents that relate to construction or capital
projects shall cite this chapter.  (Ord. 19402 § 14, 2022).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<h1><a name="_18.19_COMMERCIAL_PROPERTY"></a><span style='font-size:12.0pt'>18.19  COMMERCIAL
PROPERTY ASSESSED CLEAN ENERGY AND RESILIENCY FINANCING PROGRAM</span></h1>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>Sections:</span></b></p>

<p class=tx style='margin-left:1.5in;text-indent:-1.5in'><span
style='font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          18.19.010     Definitions.</span></p>

<p class=tx style='margin-left:1.5in;text-indent:-1.5in'><span
style='font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          18.19.020     Program
established - requirements - voluntary assessments - liens - recording -
collection of payments, enforcement of delinquencies, foreclosure - compliance
with chapter 36.165 RCW - appeals - reports.</span></p>

<p class=tx style='margin-left:1.5in;text-indent:-1.5in'><span
style='font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          18.19.030     Written
consent from lien, mortgage, or security interest holder - written consent from
affordable housing covenants, restrictions or regulatory agreements holders -
fee when executive or designee acts as program administrator.</span></p>

<p class=tx style='margin-left:1.5in;text-indent:-1.5in'><span
style='font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          18.19.040     Restrictions
on King County.</span></p>

<p class=tx style='margin-left:1.5in;text-indent:-1.5in'><span
style='font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          18.19.050     Members
of council, executive, and county employees not personally liable.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          18.19.010  Definitions. 
</span></b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>The definitions in this section apply throughout this
chapter unless the context clearly requires otherwise.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          A. 
&quot;Assessment&quot; means the voluntary agreement of a property owner to
allow the county to place  an  annual assessment on the owner's property to
repay financing.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          B. 
&quot;Capital provider&quot; means any private entity or the entity's designee,
successor or assign, that makes or provides financing under this chapter.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          C. 
&quot;C-PACER Program Guidebook&quot; means a comprehensive document that
designates the applicable region for the program and establishes guidelines,
specifications and processes and contains standard application forms and other
documents consistent with the administration of the program.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          D. 
&quot;Eligible property&quot; means privately owned commercial, industrial or
agricultural real property or multifamily residential real property with five
or more dwelling units.  &quot;Eligible property&quot; may be owned by any type
of business, corporation, individual or nonprofit organization permitted by
state law.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          E. 
&quot;Financing&quot; means an investment from a capital provider to a property
owner to finance or refinance a qualified project.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          F. 
&quot;Financing agreement&quot; means the contract under which a property owner
agrees to repay a capital provider for financing including, but not limited to,
details of any finance charges, fees, debt servicing, accrual of interest,
accrual of penalties and any terms relating to treatment of prepayment and
partial payment of the financing.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          G.  &quot;Fossil
fuel&quot; means fossil fuels as defined in K.C.C. 21.A.06.532C.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          H. 
&quot;Lien&quot; means the lien recorded at the county on the eligible property
to secure the voluntary annual assessment, which remains on the property until
paid in full.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          I. 
&quot;Program&quot; means a commercial property assessed clean energy and
resiliency program established under this chapter.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          J. 
&quot;Program administrator&quot; means the party designated by the county to
administer the program.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          K. 
&quot;Project application&quot; means an application submitted to the county to
demonstrate that a proposed project qualifies for financing and for a lien.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          L. 
&quot;Qualified improvement&quot; means a permanent improvement affixed to real
property.  &quot;Qualified improvement&quot; includes at least one of the
following:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            1.  An
energy-efficiency improvement, which means it decreases electricity consumption
or demand or reduces greenhouse gas emissions through the use of efficiency
technologies, products or activities that reduce or support the reduction of
electricity consumption, or that it allows for the reduction in electricity
demand.  Energy efficiency improvements shall not include the installation,
maintenance or repair of equipment that burns fossil fuels;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            2.  An
electrification improvement, which means it eliminates the combustion of fossil
fuels through the electrification of space or water heating;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.  Electric
vehicle charging infrastructure, which means it supports the electrification of
the transportation sector and the reduction of greenhouse gas emissions;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            4.  A
renewable energy improvement, which means it supports the production of a
clean, renewable resource as defined in RCW 19.405.020(34), including, but not
limited to a product, device or interacting group of products or devices on the
customer's side of the meter that generates electricity, provides thermal
energy or regulates temperature;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            5.  A water
conservation improvement, which means it decreases water consumption or demand
through the use of efficiency technologies, products or activities that reduce
or support the reduction of water consumption, or allow for the reduction in
demand, or reduces or eliminates lead from water that might be used for
drinking or cooking through the use of technologies, products, or activities
that address safe drinking water; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            6.  A
resilience improvement, which means it increases building or community
resilience, including but not limited to seismic retrofits, flood mitigation,
stormwater management, fire detection and suppression, wildfire and wind
resistance, energy storage and microgrids that reduce public risk and emergency
response.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          M. 
&quot;Qualified project&quot; means a project approved by the program
administrator, involving the installation or modification of a qualified
improvement, including new construction or the adaptive reuse of eligible
property with a qualified improvement.  (Ord. 19360 § 2, 2021).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          18.19.020  Program
established - requirements - voluntary assessments - liens - recording -
collection of payments, enforcement of delinquencies, foreclosure - compliance
with chapter 36.165 RCW - appeals - reports.</span></b></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>          A.  The executive shall establish a
commercial property assessed clean energy and resiliency program, commonly
known as a C-PACER program, available in the entire jurisdiction of King
County, including both unincorporated and incorporated areas.  The program
shall allow owners of agricultural, commercial and industrial properties and of
multifamily residential properties with five or more dwelling units, to obtain
low-cost, long-term financing for qualified improvements from private capital
providers.  Qualified projects must be located wholly within the jurisdiction
of King County.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>          B.  The executive shall establish the
program substantially in the form of Attachment A to Ordinance 19449*, the King
County C-PACER Program Guide.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>          C.  The qualifying improvements will
be repaid through a voluntary assessment on the property, secured by a county
lien, and assigned to a capital provider for all the administrative aspects of
billing, collecting, and enforcing the lien.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>          D.  The county shall have no
obligation to prosecute the foreclosure of a C-PACER lien on behalf of the
capital provider, and the capital provider, by being a party to the assessment
agreement, accepting an assignment of a C-PACER lien in accordance with the
assignment of the notice of assessment and assessment agreement, shall be
responsible for the collection payments and enforcement of any delinquencies. 
The capital provider may foreclose the C-PACER lien at any time after one year
from the date of delinquency of an assessment payment in the same manner as a
mortgage lien under chapter 61.12 RCW except that a sale of the property shall
not discharge or in any manner affect the priority of the C-PACER lien with
respect to installments not yet due and payable at the time of sale, and no
deficiency judgment may be sought by the capital provider with respect to any
unpaid assessment at the time of sale.  The participation of the county sheriff
in any such a foreclosure action shall not be deemed a violation of or
inconsistent with RCW 36.165.110 and K.C.C. 18.19.040.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>          E.  In accordance with RCW
36.165.030, the program must begin accepting applications and approving
applications no later than six months after December 10, 2021.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>          F.  In accordance with RCW
36.165.050, the executive or designee shall record each lien in the real
property records of the county.  The recording must include:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            1.  The legal description of the
eligible property;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            2.  The assessor's parcel number of
the property;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            3.  The grantor's name, which must
be the same as the property owner on the assessment agreement;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            4.  The grantee's name, which must
be King County;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            5.  The date on which the lien was
created;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            6.  The principal amount of the
lien;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            7.  The terms and length of the
lien; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            8.  A copy of the voluntary
assessment agreement between the county and the property owner.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>          G.  The executive shall also record
the assignment of the lien from the county to the appropriate capital provider.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>          H.  The executive shall ensure that
the program complies with chapter 36.165 RCW.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>          I.  Denial of an application may be
appealed to the office of the hearing examiner by filing an appeal with the
hearing examiner.  K.C.C. 20.22.080.B. through G. shall govern the appeal
process.  The examiner shall issue a final decision in accordance with K.C.C.
20.22.040.  The examiner may adopt reasonable rules or regulations for
conducting its business.  The executive or designee shall make the rules freely
accessible to the public.  The fee for filing an appeal under this subsection
shall be the same as required by K.C.C. 4A.780.010.  The fee shall be paid at
the time the appeal statement is delivered and is not refundable.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>          J.1.  The executive shall provide a
report two years after beginning to accept applications, and every two years
following.  The report shall include:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              a.  the number of project
applications received and processed;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              b.  the total value of project
applications received and processed; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              c.  the estimated energy and
water savings and renewable energy deployed from projects, and the number of
resiliency measures financed.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            2.  The executive should
electronically file the report required by this section with the clerk of the
council, who shall retain an electronic copy and provide an electronic copy to
all councilmembers.  (Ord. 19449 § 2, 2022:  Ord. 19360 § 3, 2021).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>*Available in the King County Archives.</span></b></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>          <b>18.19.030  Written consent from
lien, mortgage, or security interest holder - written consent from affordable
housing covenants, restrictions or regulatory agreements holders - fee when
executive or designee acts as program administrator.</b></span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>          A.  Before a capital provider may
enter into a financing agreement to provide financing of a qualified project to
the owner of record of any eligible property, the capital provider must receive
written consent from any holder of a lien, mortgage, or security interest in
the real property that the property may participate in the program and that the
lien will take precedence over all other liens except for a lien for taxes as
described in RCW 36.165.060.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>          B.  Before a capital provider may
enter into a financing agreement to provide financing of a qualified project to
the owner of record of any multifamily residential real property with five or
more dwelling units, the program administrator must also receive written
consent from any and all holders of affordable housing covenants, restrictions
or regulatory agreements in the real property that the property may participate
in the program and that the lien will take precedence over all other liens
except for a lien for taxes as described in RCW 36.165.060.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>          C.  The executive or designee may
impose a fee equal to one percent of the total cost of the qualified project or
fifteen thousand dollars, whichever is less, when acting as the program
administrator.  (Ord. 19360 § 4, 2021).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>          <b>18.19.040  Restrictions on King
County</b>.  King County, in conjunction with the program, may not:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>          A.  Make the issuance of a permit,
license, or other authorization from the county to a person who owns property
in the region contingent on the person entering into a written contract to
repay the financing of a qualified project under chapter 36.165. RCW;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>          B.  Compel a person who owns property
in the region to enter into a written contract to repay the financing of a
qualified project under chapter 36.165 RCW; or</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>          C.  Enforce any privately financed
debt by a capital provider under this chapter or use public moneys to support
or repay any loan between a capital provider and property owner.  (Ord. 19360 §
5, 2021).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>          <b>18.19.050  Members of council,
executive, and county employees not personally liable.</b>  The members of the
council, the executive, and county employees are not personally liable as a
result of exercising any rights or responsibilities conveyed by chapter 36.165
RCW and this chapter.  (Ord. 19360 § 6, 2021).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<h1><a name="_Toc32491848"><span style='font-size:12.0pt'>18.20  SUSTAINABLE
PURCHASE POLICY</span></a></h1>

<p class=MsoNormal align=center style='text-align:center'><b><i><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>(Formerly
</span></i></b><b><i><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>ENVIRONMENTALLY
PREFERABLE PRODUCT PROCUREMENT POLICY)</span></i></b></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>Sections:</span></b></p>

<p class=tx><span style='font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          18.20.010     Purpose.</span></p>

<p class=tx><span style='font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          18.20.015     Definitions.</span></p>

<p class=tx><span style='font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          18.20.020     Policies.</span></p>

<p class=tx><span style='font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          18.20.025     Evaluation
of independent third-party standards – establishment of                                    minimum
standards.</span></p>

<p class=tx><span style='font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          18.20.070     Responsibilities
of departments.</span></p>

<p class=tx><span style='font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          18.20.080     Solid
waste division responsibilities.</span></p>

<p class=tx><span style='font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          18.20.090     Procurement
and payables section responsibilities.</span></p>

<p class=tx><span style='font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          18.20.100     Exemptions.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          18.20.010 
Purpose.</span></b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>  The purpose of this chapter is to ensure that King
County agencies purchase sustainable goods and services whenever they meet the
price and performance requirements of the county and advance the goals and
priority actions of the King County Strategic Climate Action Plan, Green
Building Ordinance and Equity and Social Justice Strategic Plan.  (Ord. 19540 §
14, 2022:  Ord. 18750 § 1, 2018:  Ord. 14811 § 26, 2003:  Ord. 9240 § 1, 1989. 
Formerly K.C.C. 10.16.010).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=Normal style='text-align:justify'><span style='letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          <b>18.20.015 
Definitions.</b>  </span>The definitions in this section apply throughout this
chapter unless the context clearly requires otherwise.</p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>          A.  &quot;Federal guidance&quot;
means the United States Environmental Protection Agency's comprehensive
purchasing guidelines authorized by Congress under section 6002 of the Resource
Conservation and Recovery Act, 42 U.S.C. 6962, as amended, and guidelines for
performance standards and ecolabels.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>          B.  &quot;Social cost of carbon&quot;
means expected economic costs created from net changes in agricultural
productivity, human health, property damage from increased flood risk, and the
value of ecosystem services caused by carbon emissions and climate change.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>          C.  &quot;Sustainable goods and
services&quot; for the purposes of this chapter, &quot;goods&quot; shall have
the same meaning as &quot;intangible personal property&quot; as defined in
K.C.C. chapter 2.93, such that &quot;goods&quot; include materials, products,
supplies, or equipment supplied by a contractor and &quot;services&quot; shall
have the same meanings as &quot;services,&quot; &quot;technical services&quot;
and &quot;professional services&quot; as defined in K.C.C. chapter 2.93, such
that &quot;services&quot; include labor, work, analysis, or similar activities
supplied by a contractor to accomplish a specific scope of work.  To be
&quot;sustainable,&quot; the good or service must also</span><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'> </span><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>provide environmental,
social, and economic benefits while protecting public health and environment
over the entire life cycle of the good or service, from the extraction of raw
materials through final disposal.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>          D.  &quot;Sustainable
purchasing&quot; means procuring sustainable goods and services in a manner
that integrate fiscal responsibility, social equity and community and
environmental stewardship.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>          E.  &quot;Total life-cycle cost&quot;
means the comprehensive accounting of the total cost of ownership, including
initial costs, energy and operational costs, longevity and efficacy of service,
and disposal costs.  </span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>(Ord. 18750 § 2, 2018).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          18.20.020 
Policies.</span></b></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          A.  In
accordance with this chapter, departments shall purchase sustainable goods and
services whenever they meet price, performance and availability requirements.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>          B.  Departments shall implement
sustainable purchasing by including in their procurement planning process, for
all goods and services purchased in accordance with K.C.C. chapter 2.93,
consideration of the factors and strategies in subsections C. through F. of
this section as either performance or responsibility criteria and adherence
with subsection G. of this section.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>          C.  Environmental factors to be
considered when purchasing goods and services include, but are not limited to:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            1.  Recycled content;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            2.  Pollutant releases;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            3.  Toxicity, especially the use of
persistent, bioaccumulative and toxic chemicals;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            4.  Waste generation;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            5.  Greenhouse gas emissions, which
also factors in the location and transportation of goods;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            6.  Energy and water consumption;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            7.  Depletion or degradation of
natural resources;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            8.  Impacts on biodiversity and
habitat;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            9.  Social cost of carbon and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            10.  Use of local businesses.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>          D.  Social equity factors to be
considered when purchasing goods and services include, but are not limited to:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            1.  Human health impacts;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            2.  Fair wage, also known as living
wage, in accordance with K.C.C. chapter 3.18; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            3.  Use of small contractors and
suppliers, in accordance with K.C.C. chapter 2.97.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>          E.  Fiscal factors to be considered
when purchasing goods and services include, but are not limited to:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            1.  Use reduction;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            2.  Product or material
performance, quality, durability and repairability;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            3.  Life-cycle cost assessment;
lowest total life-cycle cost;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            4.  Leveraging buying power;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            5.  Impact on county staff time and
labor; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            6.  Long-term financial or other
market changes.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>          F.  Departments shall promote and
encourage strategies to reduce consumption, thus lessening the impact on
landfills, resource depletion, impact on human health and greenhouse gas
emissions.  The strategies may include, but are not limited to:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            1.  Procuring goods delivered in
minimal packaging;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            2.  Reducing the procurement of
goods that contain and perpetuate the use of toxics;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            3.  Procuring goods that are
durable, long-lasting, reusable, recyclable, repairable, remanufactured or
refurbished or that otherwise create less waste; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            4.  Any one or more of
manufacturer, vendor and municipal take-back programs.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          G.  In those
contracts for which a department utilized sustainable purchasing, the county
shall require its selected goods or services contractor or consultant to use sustainable
goods and services and minimize waste whenever practicable in the fulfillment
of the contract.  (Ord. 18750 § 3, 2018:  Ord. 15912 § 29, 2007:  Ord. 14811 §
27, 2003:  Ord. 9240 § 2, 1989.  Formerly K.C.C. 10.16.020).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          <b>18.20.025 
Evaluation of independent third-party standards – establishment of minimum
standards.</b></span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>          A.  Departments are required to
evaluate the use of independent, third-party environmental good or service
label standards when applicable when writing specifications for or procuring
goods or services, to ensure that they are sustainable, so long as the labels:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            1.  Were developed and awarded by
an impartial third-party; </span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            2.  Were developed in a public,
transparent, and broad stakeholder process; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            3.  Satisfy the standards for
certification developed by the International Organization of Standards or other
recognized standards-setting or accreditation organizations.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>          B.  The procurement and payables
section of the finance and business operations division, in consultation with
departments, shall establish minimum environmental standards for goods and
services in accordance with Strategic Climate Action Plan goals, measures and
targets, federal guidance, third-party certified environmental standards and
best practices.  </span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>(Ord. 18750 § 4, 2018).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          18.20.070 
Responsibilities of departments.</span></b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>  All departments are
responsible for:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          A.  Assigning
appropriate personnel to evaluate opportunities for buying sustainable goods
and services reflected in federal guidance or communicated by the procurement
and payables section of the finance and business operations division through
its sustainable purchasing implementation guidance document, other tools and
training for departments;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          B.  Purchasing sustainable
goods and services whenever practicable and as required or recommended by the
sustainable purchasing implementation guidance document;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          C.  Working with
the procurement and payables section of the finance and business operations
division to set appropriate environmental standards and certifications for
various goods categories, conducting sustainable purchasing training, including
encouraging employee attendance at internal and external trainings related to
sustainable purchasing;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          D.  Ensuring
internal policies and procedures provide for the implementation of this
chapter;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          E.  Encouraging
pilot testing for sustainable goods and services; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          F.  Ensuring
contractors and consultants use and supply sustainable goods and services and
minimize waste whenever practicable.  (Ord. 18750 § 6, 2018:  Ord. 17074 § 3,
2011:  Ord. 15912 § 32, 2007:  Ord. 14811 § 32, 2003:  Ord. 9240 § 16, 1989. 
Formerly K.C.C. 10.16.160).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          18.20.080  Solid
waste division responsibilities.</span></b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>  The solid waste division
is responsible for:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          A.  Providing
information and technical assistance to external agencies, such as local
governments, schools, colleges and other public and private organizations to
increase their purchase of sustainable goods and services; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          B.  Assisting
the procurement and payables section of the finance and business operations
division in fulfilling its responsibilities under this chapter as related to
recycled materials and products, waste reduction, green building, toxics
reduction and recycling.  (Ord. 18750 § 7, 2018:  Ord. 17166 § 12, 2011:  Ord.
17074 § 4, 2011:  Ord. 15912 § 33, 2007:  Ord. 9240 § 17, 1989.  Formerly
K.C.C. 10.16.170).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          18.20.090 
Procurement and payables section responsibilities.</span></b><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'> 
The procurement and payables section of the finance and business operations
division is responsible for:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          A.  Assigning
appropriate personnel to fulfill the requirements of this policy;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          B.  Assisting
agencies in developing sustainable purchasing specifications and integrating
these requirements into contracting practices;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          C.  Developing
and maintaining an implementation guidance document, training and other tools
to assist agencies in specifying and buying sustainable goods and services;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          D.  Researching
opportunities for procurement of sustainable goods and services by communicating
these to appropriate county departments for evaluation and purchase through the
implementation guidance document, website and training;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          E.  Establishing
minimum environmental standards for goods and services and setting priorities
in accordance with Strategic Climate Action Plan goals, measures and targets,
state and federal guidance, third-party certified environmental standards and
best practices;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          F.  Providing
education and training to agencies to support implementation of this chapter;
and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          G.  Collecting
purchase data, preparing information and providing staff support as necessary
for the preparation of the report required in K.C.C. 18.50.010.  (Ord. 18750 § 8,
2018:  Ord. 17166 § 14, 2011:  Ord. 17085 § 4, 2011:  Ord. 14811 § 35, 2003: 
Ord. 9240 § 18, 1989.  Formerly K.C.C. 10.16.180).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          18.20.100 
Exemptions.</span></b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>  Nothing in this chapter shall be construed as requiring
a department or contractor to procure products that do not perform adequately
for their intended end use or are not available at a reasonable price in a
reasonable period of time.  (Ord. 9240 § 19, 1989.  Formerly K.C.C. 10.16.190).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<h1><a name="_Toc32491849"><span style='font-size:12.0pt'>18.22  ELECTRIC
VEHICLES FOR COUNTY</span></a></h1>

<p class=Normal style='text-align:justify'>&nbsp;</p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>Sections:</span></b></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          18.22.010     <a
name="_Hlk32491658">Electric vehicles – duties of departments and divisions –
evaluation </a>–                      implementation – duties, reports and
encouraged actions.</span></p>

<p class=Normal style='text-align:justify'>&nbsp;</p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          <b>18.22.010  Electric
vehicles – duties of departments and divisions – evaluation – implementation –
duties, reports and encouraged actions.</b></span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify;text-autospace:none'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>          A.  The
executive shall implement a &quot;jump start&quot; vehicle electrification
strategy that seeks to accelerate the adoption of electric vehicles by the
Metro transit department, by other county agencies and by residents.  The
strategy shall include goals and actions across county agencies.  The following
King County vehicle electrification goals are established:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify;text-autospace:none'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            1.  A
one-hundred-percent zero-emission revenue bus fleet by 2035;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify;text-autospace:none'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>          <b>  </b>2. 
A sixty-seven-percent zero-emission ADA paratransit fleet by 2040;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify;text-autospace:none'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            3.  A forty-percent
zero-emission rideshare fleet by 2030 and one-hundred-percent zero-emission
rideshare fleet by 2040;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify;text-autospace:none'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            4.  Fifty
percent of light-duty vehicles are transitioned to electric by 2030 and one
hundred percent by 2035;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify;text-autospace:none'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            5.  Fifty
percent of medium-duty vehicles are transitioned to zero-emission by 2035 and
one hundred percent by 2040;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify;text-autospace:none'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            6.  Fifty
percent of heavy-duty vehicles are transitioned to zero-emission vehicles by 2040
and one hundred percent by 2045;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify;text-autospace:none'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            7. 
Installation of charging stations that provide charging ports capable of
simultaneously charging one hundred fifty fleet vehicles by 2030 in county
facilities; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify;text-autospace:none'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            8. 
Installation of public</span> <span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>charging
stations that provide charging ports capable of simultaneously charging one
hundred twenty-five vehicles by 2030.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify;text-autospace:none'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>          B.  The
department of executive services shall:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify;text-autospace:none'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            1.  Require
that all new light-duty vehicles purchased are electric, unless the customer
agency can demonstrate that there are no feasible alternatives that meet
business needs and support delivery of county services; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify;text-autospace:none'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            2.  Work
with county agencies managing medium- and heavy-duty vehicles to seek grant
funding and collaborate with utilities and manufacturers to pilot use of medium
and heavy-duty electric vehicles in support of achieving electrification of
fifty percent of the medium-duty fleet by 2035 and fifty percent of the
heavy-duty fleet by 2040.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify;text-autospace:none'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>          C.  The Metro
transit department should reduce the overall carbon emissions from
transportation as quickly as possible while achieving the goals of Metro
Connects.  If there are alternative approaches to the vehicle electrification
goals specified in subsection A. of this section that would either more quickly
reduce overall greenhouse gas emissions, including greenhouse gas emissions and
diesel particulate matter in the community, or that would achieve similar
greenhouse gas emission and diesel particulate matter reductions, the executive
should pursue those approaches in lieu of or in addition to specified vehicle
electrification goals in subsection A. of this section.  Before the executive
initiates an alternative approach, the executive shall notify the council and
include a description of any deviation from the vehicle electrification goals
specified in subsection A. of this section, as well as include information
about the alternative approach.  The notification required by this subsection
C. shall be transmitted as part of a report developed under K.C.C. 18.50.010 or
electronically filed as a standalone the report with the clerk of the council,
who shall retain an electronic copy and provide an electronic copy to all
councilmembers, the council chief of staff, and the lead staff for the
transportation, economy, and environment committee, or its successor.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify;text-autospace:none'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>          D.  The parks
and recreation division shall increase the number of electric vehicle charging
ports at King County-operated parks to support electrification of county fleet
vehicles and increase public access in areas with limited access to electric
vehicle charging.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify;text-autospace:none'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>          E.  When
evaluating public charging infrastructure needs, the executive shall consider
the charging infrastructure required for a range of electric vehicles including
light electric vehicles such as electric bikes, scooters, or other
battery-powered vehicles used for transporting people from one place to
another.  The executive shall monitor trends in electric vehicle adoption and
develop consistent policies and practices for public electric charging at King
County facilities.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify;text-autospace:none'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>          F.  The
executive shall implement this section consistent with K.C.C. 4A.700.700.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify;text-autospace:none'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>          G.1.  To
support the goals in subsections A. through F. of this section, King County,
including the Metro transit department and the department of executive services
shall engage with stakeholders, such as the King County climate and equity
community taskforce.  Stakeholder engagement will support efforts of local
jurisdictions, regional governments, employers, charging infrastructure
companies, utilities and environmental and climate justice groups accelerate
the adoption of electric vehicles while ensuring the equitable distribution of
benefits of electric vehicles and promoting equitable access to mobility that
prioritizes shared mobility solutions. King County will also participate in
regional forums to support and continue to advocate for funding and enabling
legislation.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify;text-autospace:none'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            2.  The
executive shall transmit to the council revisions to the King County Code that
facilitate the electrification of county fleets.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify;text-autospace:none'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            3.  The
executive shall partner with utilities and community organizations on a pilot
program to facilitate access to electric vehicles and electric vehicle
infrastructure, including shared mobility services, by low-income residents of
King County.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify;text-autospace:none'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            4.  The
executive shall develop policies to encourage the adoption of electric vehicles
by transportation network companies.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify;text-autospace:none'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            5.  The
executive shall work with cities within King County to share best practices and
policies for encouraging the adoption of electric vehicles for their fleet and
by residents.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            6.  The executive is encouraged to
submit appropriations to the county council that will allow for installation of
charging infrastructure for public use where legally permissible at county
facilities used by the public.  The executive , in coordination with utilities,
is also encouraged to submit appropriations to the county council that include
incentive programs to support the installation of at-home charging
infrastructure in single and multifamily homes, including affordable housing
projects where the department of community and human services has investment,
with priority for funding given to providing charging infrastructure to
low-income households.  <span style='letter-spacing:-.1pt'>(Ord. 19986 § 6,
2025:  Ord. 19052 § 2, 2020).</span></span></p>

<p class=Normal>&nbsp;</p>

<h1><a name="_Hlk32491351"></a><a name="_Toc32491850"><span style='font-size:
12.0pt'>18.25  STRATEGIC CLIMATE ACTION PLAN</span></a></h1>

<p class=Normal style='text-align:justify'>&nbsp;</p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>Sections:</span></b></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          18.25.010      Strategic
climate action plan updates.</span></p>

<p class=Normal style='text-align:justify'>&nbsp;</p>

<p class=Normal style='text-align:justify'>          <b>18.25.010  Strategic
climate action plan updates.</b></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          A.  In order to
guide the county's climate-related objectives and strategies, the executive
shall develop an updated strategic climate action plan at least every five
years.  Each update to the strategic climate action plan shall be developed
with an environmental justice framework in partnership with those communities
disproportionately impacted by climate change and in a manner consistent with K.C.C.
2.10.200, 2.10.210, 2.10.220, and 2.10.230.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          B.  The
strategic climate action plan shall include, but not be limited to, the elements
listed in this subsection B.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              1.a.  A
reducing greenhouse gas emissions section that includes the identification of
specific goals, performance measures and related targets, and actions, at both
the community scale and county operational scale, to advance the countywide goal
of reducing greenhouse gas emissions fifty percent by 2030, seventy-five
percent by 2040, and ninety-five percent by 2050, with net-zero emissions
through carbon sequestration and other strategies by that year, compared to a
2007 baseline.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              b.  The reducing
greenhouse gas emissions section shall, at minimum, address reducing emissions
in the following areas: transportation and land use; building and facilities
energy; green building and sustainable infrastructure; consumption and
materials management; forestry and agriculture; and zero-emission county fleets.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              c.  Green
building performance measures shall include percentage of King County-owned
capital projects achieving the highest green building standard specified in
K.C.C. 18.17.050, and percentage of construction and demolition materials
diverted from landfills.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              d. 
Zero-emission county fleet performance measures shall align with the &quot;jump
start&quot; vehicle electrification strategy in K.C.C. 18.22.010.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify;text-autospace:none'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            2.  A
community-driven sustainable and resilient frontline communities section that
includes identification of specific goals, performance measures and related
targets, and actions to achieve sustainable and resilient communities.  The sustainable
and resilient frontline communities section shall:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify;text-autospace:none'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              a. 
identify how climate change will impact communities of color, low-income
communities, and those disproportionately impacted by climate change;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify;text-autospace:none'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              b. 
identify opportunities to take actions to address those impacts that could
include increasing the number of affordable housing units, developing pathways
to green jobs, preventing neighborhood displacement, increasing access to
affordable energy efficiency and decarbonization, increasing access to green
spaces, providing access to zero emissions mobility options, improving food
security, reducing pollution and addressing health disparities; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify;text-autospace:none'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              c.  based
on assessment of climate impacts and extreme weather events like heat waves on
vulnerable communities, make recommendations for preparedness strategies and
actions to include in county emergency response plans, the flood management
plan, and the regional hazard mitigation plan.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.  A climate
preparedness section that includes the current assessment of climate change
impacts in King County and identification of specific goals, performance
measures and related targets, and actions, actions at both the community scale
and county operational scale, to address climate change impacts.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            4.a.  A
climate and workforce strategy that emphasizes King County's dedication to
ensuring greater access to career and economic opportunities stemming from
climate action efforts, including, but not limited to, the strategic climate
action plan.  The intent of the climate and workforce strategy is to underscore
the interconnectedness of climate initiatives and workforce and economic
development, ensuring that both objectives can progress in tandem for the
benefit of all county residents across the career spectrum.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              b.  The
climate and workforce strategy shall be developed in consultation with members
of the King County climate equity community task force identified in subsection
E. of this section, the labor advisory council and labor and workforce
development organizations identified in subsection G. of this section,
representatives of an environmental justice and climate equity organization,
education and training providers, local businesses and contractors invested in
clean energy deployment, building managers, utilities, tribes, local
governments, and regional groups such as the King County-Cities Climate
Collaboration and the Puget Sound Regional Council, and shall:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>                (1) 
include specific actions King County and its partners can take to increase
clear pathways for members of historically underserved communities affected by
climate change, informed by recommendations of the climate equity community
task force, to access living wage roles providing environmental benefits, such
as clean energy deployment, in high-demand industry sectors of construction,
manufacturing, transportation, professional services and technology;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>                (2) 
identify opportunities to increase the number of sustainability and energy
conservation career opportunities throughout the region, including jobs in
energy efficiency, renewable energy, electric vehicles, and carbon
sequestration, and King County administrative, executive, policy, and technical
jobs;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>                (3) 
include a proposal for and budget to develop career pathways that focuses
especially on communities that have historically been underserved, and is
informed by recommendations of the climate equity community task force;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>                (4) 
identify the industry sectors and job types providing a living wage and
environmental benefits, such as clean energy deployment, in King County every
ten years; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>                (5) 
actions King County can take to increase the sustainability and energy
conservation credentials of King County's own workforce, such as collaboration
on development of apprenticeship and preapprenticeship programs in sectors
including, but not limited to, energy efficiency, electrification, electric
vehicle maintenance, and the maintenance of electric vehicle infrastructure.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            5.  The
reporting required by K.C.C 18.50.010.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          C.  Each goal
and action identified in the strategic climate action plan shall address
environmental justice and ensure that the action promotes an equitable
distribution of any environmental benefit.  The strategic climate action plan
should establish explicit and, whenever possible, quantifiable connections
between the overarching climate goals and specific actions.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              D.  Performance
measures and related targets required by this section shall advance the
strategic climate action plan and provide for assessment of progress relative
to overarching climate goals at the community scale</span> <span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>and
county operational scale.  Progress in achieving the performance measure
targets shall be reported on a publicly accessible climate performance tracking
dashboard.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          E.  In order to
achieve a community-driven strategic climate action plan, the executive shall
convene and partner with the King County climate equity community task force to
develop the sustainable and resilient frontline communities section and to
consult on other sections of the strategic climate action plan.  The King
County climate equity community task force shall be a racially and ethnically
diverse group representing various communities in King County that are on the
frontline of climate change.  The task force shall develop goals and guide
priority areas for climate action based on community values and concerns.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          F.  In
developing updates to the strategic climate action plan, the executive shall continue
to review climate change-related plans being developed by other municipalities,
including the city of Seattle's climate action plan, and identify opportunities
and strengthen recommendations for partnership with cities, businesses, and
nonprofit organizations to advance actions in all areas of the strategic
climate action plan.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            G.</span><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>  The executive shall
convene a strategic climate action plan labor advisory council and seek input
from county labor and workforce development organizations, including the Martin
Luther King, Jr. County Labor Council of Washington, the Seattle Building and
Construction Trades Council, the Workforce Development Council of Seattle-King
County, and the Coalition for Climate Careers, on recommendations for policies,
programs, and partnerships to strengthen career pathways and to provide
guidance on each update.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify;text-autospace:none'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            H.  The
executive shall consult with Indian tribes, and shall collaborate with cities
in King County through the King County-Cities Climate Collaboration, on each
update to the strategic climate action plan.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          I.  Future
updates to climate-related objectives and strategies should be informed by the most-recently
adopted strategic climate action plan.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          J.  The
executive shall transmit updates to the strategic climate action plan to the
council for acceptance by motion.  The executive shall electronically file the
legislation with the clerk of the council, who shall retain an electronic copy
and provide an electronic copy to all councilmembers, the council chief of
staff, and the lead staff for the transportation, economy, and environment
committee or its successor.  (Ord. 19986 § 7, 2025:  Ord. 19881 § 29, 2024:  Ord.
19820 § 6, 2024:  Ord. 19540 § 15, 2022:  Ord. 19041 § 1, 2019:  Ord. 18392 § 1,
2016:  Ord. 17270 § 2, 2012).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<h1><span style='font-size:12.0pt'>18.30  COMPOST PROCUREMENT AND USE</span></h1>

<p class=open style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=open style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>Sections:</span></b></p>

<p class=tx style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-indent:-1.0in'><span
style='font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>18.30.010     Definition
- &quot;compost.&quot;</span></p>

<p class=tx style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-indent:-1.0in'><span
style='font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>18.30.020     Consideration
and planning for use of compost - exceptions - prioritization of certain
companies - annual reports to solid waste division.</span></p>

<p class=tx style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-indent:-1.0in'><span
style='font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>18.30.030     Solid
waste division responsibilities - technical assistance, educational outreach,
reporting of organic material diverted.</span></p>

<p class=tx style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-indent:-1.0in'><span
style='font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>18.30.040     Solid
waste division responsibilities - biennial report to Washington state
Department of Ecology.</span></p>

<p class=tx style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-indent:-1.0in'><span
style='font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>18.30.050     Business
operations division responsibilities - collaboration with departments - assist
solid waste division's reporting.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          <b>18.30.010  Definition
- &quot;compost.&quot;</b>  For the purposes of this chapter, unless the
context clearly requires otherwise, &quot;compost&quot; means a product created
with &quot;composted material&quot; as defined in RCW 70A.205.015(3). 
&quot;Compost&quot; includes, but is not limited to, one hundred percent
finished compost or blends that include compost as a primary ingredient.  Mulch
is &quot;compost&quot; if it contains a minimum of sixty percent composted
material.  Bark is not &quot;compost.&quot;  (Ord. 19552 § 2, 2022)</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          <b>18.30.020  Consideration
and planning for use of compost - exceptions - prioritization of certain
companies - annual reports to solid waste division.</b></span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          A.  Before
issuance of a solicitation for bids or proposals in accordance with K.C.C.
chapter 2.93, departments shall consider whether compost can be utilized in a
county project as required in RCW 43.19A.120.  In the event that a department
identifies that compost can be utilized, that department shall purchase compost
for use in the county project.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          B.  Departments
shall plan for the use of compost in any of the following categories that are
applicable to the departments' operations and project types:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            1. 
Landscaping projects;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            2. 
Construction and postconstruction soil amendments;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3. 
Applications to prevent erosion, filter stormwater runoff, promote vegetative
growth, or improve the stability and longevity of roadways in accordance with
K.C.C. Title 9; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            4.  Low-impact
development and green infrastructure either to filter pollutants or to keep
water onsite, or both, in accordance with K.C.C. Title 9.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          C. 
Notwithstanding subsections A. and B. of this section, departments are not
required to use compost products if:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            1.  Compost
products are not available within a reasonable time or distance from the
project;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            2.  Compost
products that are available do not comply with existing purchasing standards;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.  Compost
products that are available do not comply with federal, state or local health,
quality and safety standards; or</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            4.  Compost
purchase prices are not reasonable or competitive.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          D.  Departments
shall give priority to purchasing compost products from companies that:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            1.  Produce
compost products locally;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            2.  Are
certified by the US Composting Council or an equivalent nationally recognized
organization; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.  Produce
compost products that are derived from municipal solid waste compost programs
and meet quality standards comparable to standards adopted by the Washington
state Department of Transportation or adopted by rule by the Washington state
Department of Ecology.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          E.  Departments
that use compost shall report the following information to the solid waste
division by each December 15:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            1.  The volume
and cost of compost purchased by the department in that year; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            2.  The source
or sources of the compost purchased by the department in that year.  (Ord.
19552 § 3, 2022)</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          <b>18.30.030  Solid
waste division responsibilities - technical assistance, educational outreach,
reporting of organic material diverted.</b></span>  <span style='font-size:
12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>The solid waste
division is responsible for:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          A.  Providing
technical assistance and education regarding the use of compost to King County
departments and staff;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          B.  Conducting
educational outreach to inform residents and businesses about the value of
compost and how King County uses compost in its operations each year; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          C.  Reporting
the total estimated tons of organic material diverted from the King County
waste stream because of compost use under this chapter.  (Ord. 19552 § 4, 2022)</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          <b>18.30.040  Solid
waste division responsibilities - technical assistance, educational outreach,
reporting of organic material diverted.</b></span>  <span style='font-size:
12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>The solid waste
division is responsible for compiling and submitting the report required by RCW
43.19A.150 to the Washington state Department of Ecology by December 31, 2024,
and each December 31 of even-numbered years thereafter, with the following information:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          A.  The total
tons of organic material diverted from the King County waste stream throughout
the year;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          B.  The volume
and cost of compost purchased in that reporting period by the county; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          C.  The source
or sources of the compost purchased by the county under this chapter in that
reporting period.  (Ord. 19552 § 5, 2022)</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          <b>18.30.050  Business
operations division responsibilities - collaboration with departments - assist
solid waste division's reporting.</b></span>  <span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>The finance and business
operations division is responsible for:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          A. 
Collaborating with departments to utilize specifications for compost products
in the use of sourcing and executing contracts for compost products; and </span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          B.  Assisting
the solid waste division in annual reporting by providing information about
compost contracts.  (Ord. 19552 § 6, 2022)</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<h1><span style='font-size:12.0pt'>18.35  FOREST CARBON PROGRAM</span></h1>

<p class=open style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=open style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>Sections:</span></b></p>

<p class=tx style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-indent:-1.0in'><span
style='font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>18.35.010     Definitions.</span></p>

<p class=tx style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-indent:-1.0in'><span
style='font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>18.35.020     Program
requirements - reporting.</span></p>

<p class=tx style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-indent:-1.0in'><span
style='font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>18.35.030     Exception
for council authorization of individual sales or transfers of carbon credits -
requirements.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          <b>18.35.010  Definitions.</b></span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          A. 
&quot;Additional&quot; means a carbon project creates greenhouse gas reductions
or removals over and above those that would have arisen in the absence of a
given activity and does not result from actions required by law or regulation.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          B.  &quot;Carbon
credit&quot; means the reduction, avoidance, or sequestration of one metric ton
of carbon dioxide equivalent.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          C.1. 
&quot;Carbon project&quot; means a set of actions, measures, and outcomes
specified in the applied protocol or methodology that results in additional
greenhouse gas reductions or removals.  Carbon projects are limited to the
following:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              a.  Projects
in the forestry sector, such as avoided forest conversion, improved forest
management, afforestation, or reforestation;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              b.  Projects
in the agricultural sector, such as avoided grassland conversion or improved
agricultural land management;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              c.  Other
projects that reduce or remove greenhouse gas emissions using elements of the
natural environment, such as the preservation, enhancement, or improved
management of wetlands, grasslands, or other terrestrial or aquatic ecosystems.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          2.  “Carbon
project” does not include projects that reduce or remove greenhouse gas
emissions associated with waste management processes, through artificial carbon
capture and storage technologies, or through shifts to renewable energy or
different fuel sources.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          D.  &quot;Forest
carbon program&quot; means the program administered within the water and land
resources division of the department of natural resources and parks that
manages the processes by which carbon credits are created, verified, held, and
sold or transferred.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          E. 
&quot;Protocol or methodology&quot; means a specific set of criteria and
procedures used to create a specific type of carbon project, quantify
greenhouse gas emission reductions or removals, and monitor the project.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          F. 
&quot;Retirement” or &quot;retire&quot; means the permanent removal of an
issued carbon credit from circulation.  Once a carbon credit is retired, it can
no longer be sold or transferred.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          G. 
&quot;Verified carbon credits&quot; are those carbon credits that have
undergone an independent third-party assessment of greenhouse gas emission
reductions or removals that have occurred as a result of the carbon project. 
(Ord. 19671 § 2, 2023).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          <b>18.35.020  Program
requirements - reporting.</b></span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          A.  The forest
carbon program shall create verified carbon credits through development of one
or more carbon projects.  Each carbon project shall follow the selected carbon
crediting program or greenhouse gas registry’s established protocol or
methodology. </span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          B.  The
department of natural resources and parks shall establish and maintain a system
for tracking carbon credits that clearly shows the quantity and date of carbon
credits issued to a carbon project and any sale, transfer, or retirement of
carbon credits, including the name and contact information of the recipient or
recipients of the carbon credits.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          C.1.  At the
start of each verification of carbon credits, and before the sale or transfer
of carbon credits from the carbon project, the executive shall transmit to the
council a forest carbon credit approval report and a proposed motion approving
the report.  The report shall provide details on the carbon project, including
but not limited to:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              a.  its
location;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              b.  the type
of project as described in K.C.C. 18.35.020..C.1.;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              c.  the
protocol or methodology used to verify the carbon credits; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              d.  the
number of verified credits created.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            2.  The report
may include information on multiple carbon projects.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.  The
executive shall electronically file the report and the proposed motion required
by this section with the clerk of the council.  The clerk shall retain an
electronic copy and provide an electronic copy to all councilmembers, the
council chief of staff, and the lead staff for the transportation, economy and
environment committee or its successor.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            4.  Carbon
credits from projects described in the report shall not be sold or transferred
until a motion accepting a report is passed.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          D.  The
department of natural resources and parks, subject to the requirements of this
chapter and K.C.C. 4.56.250, may sell or otherwise transfer carbon credits to
any public or private party through the forest carbon program.  Sales or
transfers may be done directly with the buyer or recipient, or may occur
through an intermediary, such as a broker or retailer.  </span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          E.  Any credits
that are sold or transferred externally shall not be used to meet the county's
operational targets or commitments.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          F.  The
department of natural resources and parks shall establish prices for carbon
credits.  Factors used to set the price may include, but shall not be limited
to:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            1.  Local,
state, national, or international carbon credit prices;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            2.  Demand for
carbon credits;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.  The cost
to create the carbon credits; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            4. 
Willingness to pay among potential buyers of the carbon credits.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          G.  The
executive shall transmit by December 31, 2025, and every two years thereafter,
a forest carbon credit sales summary report.  The report shall provide
information on carbon credits sold or transferred in the previous two years,
including but not limited to:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            1.  The total
revenue gained from carbon credit sales in the two-year period;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            2.  For each
carbon project, the number of carbon credits sold or transferred, as well as
the number of unsold carbon credits remaining;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.  
Information on each carbon credit sale or transfer, including:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              a.   all of
the project-specific information listed in subsection C.1. of this section;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              b.  the
purchaser;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              c.  the
purchase price per carbon credit;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              d.  the
documentation of the factors used by the department of natural resources and
parks to establish the sale price;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              e.  the
number of credits sold; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              f.  whether
the sale or transfer agreement was subject to council approval, or was executed
under one of the exceptions in K.C.C. 18.35.030;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            4.  A
breakdown of the percentage of carbon credit revenues that have been used in
each of the three authorized expenditure categories in K.C.C. 4A.200.455.E.4.;
and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            5.  If known,
the specific projects or programs that carbon credit revenues have funded. 
(Ord. 19671 § 3, 2023).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          <b>18.35.030  Exception
for council authorization of individual sales or transfers of carbon credits -
requirements.</b>  After the council has passed a motion approving a forest
carbon credit approval report in accordance with K.C.C. 18.35.040.C., council
authorization of individual sales or transfers of carbon credits included on an
approved carbon credit approval report is not required if:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          A.  The sale or
transfer is executed using an agreement approved by the council; or</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          B.  The sale or
transfer is executed using an agreement provided by an intermediary or a buyer
and meets the following minimum requirements:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            1.  The sale
price of the carbon credits reflects fair market value, as established  and
documented by department of natural resources and parks at the time of sale and
using the pricing factors in K.C.C. 18.35.040.F.;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            2.  The
agreement requires that the department of natural resources and parks has final
approval of the specific entity to which carbon credits are being sold or
transferred; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.  In advance
of execution, the agreement has been approved as to form by the prosecuting
attorney's office.  (Ord. 19671 § 4, 2023).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<h1><span style='font-size:12.0pt'>18.40  CARBON OFFSETS AND ENVIRONMENTAL
ATTRIBUTES PROGRAM.</span></h1>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>Sections:</span></b></p>

<p class=tx style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-indent:-99.0pt'><span
style='font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>        18.40.010     Definitions.</span></p>

<p class=tx style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-indent:-99.0pt'><span
style='font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>        18.40.020     Duties
- wastewater treatment division - solid waste division - department of natural
resources and parks - annual greenhouse gas inventory.</span></p>

<p class=tx style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-indent:-99.0pt'><span
style='font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>        18.40.030     Program
- created - administration - review of transit carbon offsets - purchase and
sale - revenue use.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=Normal style='text-align:justify'><b><span lang=DE>          18.40.010 
Definitions.</span></b><span lang=DE>  The definitions in this section apply
throughout this chapter unless the context clearly requires otherwise.</span></p>

<p class=Normal style='text-align:justify'><span lang=DE>          A. 
&quot;Additionality&quot; means the principle of achieving net greenhouse gas
emissions savings over and above those that would have arisen anyway in the
absence of a given activity or project.</span></p>

<p class=Normal style='text-align:justify'><span lang=DE>          B. 
&quot;Carbon neutral&quot; means no net greenhouse gas emissions from
operations, including when carbon offsets are applied to the emissions
calculation </span></p>

<p class=Normal style='text-align:justify'><span lang=DE>          C. 
&quot;Carbon offset&quot; means a reduction in emissions of carbon dioxide or
greenhouse gases made in order to mitigate for or to offset an emission made
elsewhere.</span></p>

<p class=Normal style='text-align:justify'><span lang=DE>          D. 
&quot;Environmental attributes&quot; means any environmental benefit that can
be monetized.</span></p>

<p class=Normal style='text-align:justify'><span lang=DE>          E. 
&quot;Renewable identification number&quot; means one of the mechanisms
established to allow obligated parties to demonstrate compliance with renewable
fuel volume obligations established under the Energy Policy Act of 2005 (Public
Law 109-58) and the Energy Independence and Security Act of 2007 (Public Law
110-140).  A renewable identification number is assigned to a unit of renewable
fuel for purposes of tracking its production and use.  Once the unit of fuel is
consumed, the renewable identification number can be used to satisfy renewable
fuel obligations and can be sold or traded to obligated parties to satisfy
their renewable fuel obligations in current or future years.</span></p>

<p class=Normal style='text-align:justify'><span lang=DE>          F.  &quot;Transit
carbon offset&quot; means an investment by the Metro transit department that
results in a reduction of greenhouse gas emissions beyond standard operations,
thereby achieving additionality.  (Ord. 18777 § 36, 2018:  Ord. 18106 § 1,
2015:  Ord. 17971 § 2, 2015).  Formerly K.C.C. 28.30.010.</span></p>

<p class=Normal style='text-align:justify'><span lang=DE>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=Normal style='text-align:justify'><span lang=DE>          <b>18.40.020 
Duties - wastewater treatment division - solid waste division - department of
natural resources and parks - annual greenhouse gas inventory.  </b>The
wastewater treatment division and the solid waste division shall aggressively
reduce both direct and indirect greenhouse gas emissions from operations.</span></p>

<p class=Normal style='text-align:justify'><span lang=DE>          The
department of natural resources and parks as a whole, and the wastewater
treatment division and the solid waste division individually, shall maintain
carbon-neutral operations.  The department and divisions should focus
greenhouse gas emissions reduction actions on projects that reduce direct
sources of greenhouse gas emissions, consistent with strategic climate action
plan actions and performance measures, and in alignment with strategic climate
action plan guidance on best practices for reducing greenhouse gas emissions. 
The reduction in greenhouse gas emissions shall be accomplished through energy
efficiency, investments in the use of renewable energy, the production of
renewable energy including solar, the use of low embodied emissions materials
in construction projects, the use of clean vehicles and fuels, the reduction of
fugitive methane emissions associated with landfills and the wastewater
treatment process, the increase of the productive use of biogas, and other projects
that reduce or sequester greenhouse gas emissions to reduce the emissions of
greenhouse emissions from operations.</span></p>

<p class=Normal style='text-align:justify'><span lang=DE>          The
wastewater treatment and solid waste divisions shall annually calculate their
greenhouse gas emissions and include those emissions totals in the strategic
climate action plan reporting of total greenhouse gas emissions from county
operations.<span style='color:#222222'>  (Ord. 19986 § 9, 2025:  Ord. 17971 §
3, 2015).  Formerly K.C.C. 28.30.020.</span></span></p>

<p class=Normal style='text-align:justify'><span lang=DE>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          18.40.030 
Program - created - administration - review of transit carbon offsets -
purchase and sale - revenue use.</span></b></p>

<p class=Normal style='text-align:justify'><span lang=DE>          A.  The King
County Metro transit carbon offset and environmental attributes program is
hereby created and shall be administered by the Metro transit department.</span></p>

<p class=Normal style='text-align:justify'><span lang=DE>          B.  Transit
carbon offsets shall be reviewed by an independent third-party organization
with proven experience in emission mitigation activities to ensure that
transitcarbon offsets meet the requirements of RCW 36.01.250.</span></p>

<p class=Normal style='text-align:justify'><span lang=DE>          C.  The Metro
transit department shall make carbon offsets or environmental attributes available
for purchase by individuals or public or private entities, if doing so is
likely to be financially beneficial to the department.</span></p>

<p class=Normal style='text-align:justify'><span lang=DE>          D.  Revenue
from the sale of carbon offsets or environmental attributes shall be used by
the Metro transit department solely for the purposes of reducing greenhouse gas
emissions through mobility services or investments that reduce the greenhouse
gas emissions from transit operations beyond standard operations, thereby
achieving additionality.</span></p>

<p class=Normal style='text-align:justify'><span lang=DE>          E.  The
executive shall ensure that transit carbon offsets or other environmental
attributes are not double counted in calculating the greenhouse gas emissions
for King County.  <span style='color:#222222'>(Ord. 19986 § 10, 2025:  Ord.
19881 § 32, 2024:  Ord. 18777 <span style='letter-spacing:-.1pt'>§</span> 37,
2018:  Ord. 18106 <span style='letter-spacing:-.1pt'>§</span> 2, 2015:  Ord.
17971 § 4, 2015).  Formerly K.C.C. 28.30.030.</span></span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<h1><a name="_Toc32491851"><span style='font-size:12.0pt'>18.50  REPORTING</span></a></h1>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>Sections:</span></b></p>

<p class=tx style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-indent:-99.0pt'><a
name="_Hlk122012811"><span style='font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:
-.1pt'>        </span></a><span style='font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>18.50.010     Progress reports.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          <b>18.50.010  Progress
reports.</b>  To track progress and prepare for each strategic climate action
plan update, the executive shall transmit a progress report to the council with
each strategic climate action plan update and at the midpoint between strategic
climate action plan updates.  The executive shall</span> <span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>electronically
file the report with the clerk of the council, who shall retain an electronic
copy and provide an electronic copy to all councilmembers, the council chief of
staff, and the lead staff for the transportation, economy and environment
committee, or its successor.  The report shall be structured in a way that
links actual performance to established goals and indicators and can inform policy
choices, program priorities, and investments in capital projects.  The report
should address the following:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          A.  Progress
towards achieving the overarching greenhouse gas emissions reduction targets at
both the community scale and county operational scale;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          B.  An update on
implementation of all goals and actions in, and progress toward meeting all
performance measure targets of, the strategic climate action plan most recently
approved by the council; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>          C.  The program
to fund city projects to reduce energy demand, as required in Ordinance 18663,
Section 3, if active.  (Ord. 19986 § 11, 2025:  Ord. 19402 § 15, 2022:  Ord. 19052
§ 3, 2020:  Ord. 19041 § 2, 2019:  Ord. 18663 § 4, 2018:  Ord. 18392 § 2, 2016: 
Ord. 17270 § 3, 2012:  Ord. 17166 § 2, 2011).<b>'</b></span></p>

</div>

